    },
    {
      "heading": "One ranking per surface",
      "body": "Instagram says it has no single algorithm: Feed, Stories, Explore and Reels each use their own, tailored to how people use that part of the app, and rank things differently [1][2][3]. Stories come from accounts a person follows, Feed mixes those with recommended posts, and Explore and Reels mostly show accounts the person does not follow [4][5][6]. Switch the surface in the panel above to see which signal moves to the top.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/instagram-ranking-explained",
          "title": "Instagram Ranking Explained (Instagram, 2023)",
          "quote": "Instagram doesn't have a singular algorithm that oversees what people do and don't see on the app"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/instagram-ranking-explained",
          "title": "Instagram Ranking Explained (Instagram, 2023)",
          "quote": "Each part of the app - Feed, Stories, Explore, Reels, Search and more - uses its own algorithm tailored to how people use it"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/instagram-ranking-explained",
          "title": "Instagram Ranking Explained (Instagram, 2023)",
          "quote": "We rank things differently in these different parts of the app"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/instagram-ranking-explained",
          "title": "Instagram Ranking Explained (Instagram, 2023)",
          "quote": "The stories you see are from people you've chosen to follow, as well as ads"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/instagram-ranking-explained",
          "title": "Instagram Ranking Explained (Instagram, 2023)",
          "quote": "your feed will have a mix of content from the accounts you've chosen to follow, recommended content from accounts we think you'll enjoy and ads"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/instagram-ranking-explained",
          "title": "Instagram Ranking Explained (Instagram, 2023)",
          "quote": "Much like Explore, the majority of what you see is from accounts you don't follow"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Signals and predictions",
      "body": "Instagram calls its inputs signals and says there are thousands, from when a post was shared to how often a person likes videos [1]. From them it makes roughly a dozen predictions of how a person will interact; in Feed the five it watches most are spending a few seconds on a post, commenting, liking, sharing and tapping the profile photo [2]. In Reels the top predictions include resharing a reel and watching it all the way through, and in Explore a post's popularity counts much more than in Feed or Stories [3][4].",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/instagram-ranking-explained",
          "title": "Instagram Ranking Explained (Instagram, 2023)",
          "quote": "there are thousands of them. They include everything from when a post was shared to whether you're using a phone or the web to how often you like videos"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/instagram-ranking-explained",
          "title": "Instagram Ranking Explained (Instagram, 2023)",
          "quote": "There are roughly a dozen of these. In Feed, the five interactions we look at most closely are how likely you are to spend a few seconds on a post, comment on it, like it, share it, and tap on the profile photo"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/instagram-ranking-explained",
          "title": "Instagram Ranking Explained (Instagram, 2023)",
          "quote": "The most important predictions we make are how likely you are to reshare a reel, watch a reel all the way through"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/instagram-ranking-explained",
          "title": "Instagram Ranking Explained (Instagram, 2023)",
          "quote": "These signals matter much more in Explore than they do in Feed or in Stories"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "What changed from 2021 to 2023",
      "body": "Instagram began in 2010 as a single stream of photos in chronological order, and by 2016 people were missing 70% of their Feed posts, which led it to rank Feed [1][2]. Its 2021 explanation gave one order for Feed and Stories, with information about the post first and your activity third [3][4]. The 2023 update gives Feed its own order with your activity first [5]. The chart below lines up the two Feed orders so each signal's move is visible.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/shedding-more-light-on-how-instagram-works",
          "title": "Shedding More Light on How Instagram Works (Instagram, 2021)",
          "quote": "When we first launched in 2010, Instagram was a single stream of photos in chronological order"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/shedding-more-light-on-how-instagram-works",
          "title": "Shedding More Light on How Instagram Works (Instagram, 2021)",
          "quote": "By 2016, people were missing 70% of all their posts in Feed, including almost half of posts from their close connections. So we developed and introduced a Feed that ranked posts"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/shedding-more-light-on-how-instagram-works",
          "title": "Shedding More Light on How Instagram Works (Instagram, 2021)",
          "quote": "The most important signals across Feed and Stories, roughly in order of importance, are: Information about the post"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/shedding-more-light-on-how-instagram-works",
          "title": "Shedding More Light on How Instagram Works (Instagram, 2021)",
          "quote": "Information about the person who posted. This helps us get a sense for how interesting the person might be to you, and includes signals like how many times people have interacted with that person in the past few weeks. Your activity."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://about.instagram.com/blog/announcements/instagram-ranking-explained",
          "title": "Instagram Ranking Explained (Instagram, 2023)",
          "quote": "The most important signals across Feed, roughly in order of importance, are: Your activity"
        }
      ]
    },
